{"mechanism_label":"custom","params":{"Vmax":9.8,"Km":1.8,"Ki":0.97,"n":2.0},"custom_code":"import numpy as np
PARAMS=['Vmax','Km','Ki','n']
def predict(df,p):
    S=np.asarray(df['S'],float); I=np.asarray(df['I'],float)
    Kapp=p['Km']*(1.0+(I/p['Ki'])**p['n'])
    return p['Vmax']*S/(Kapp+S)","confidence":0.6,"reasoning":"Vmax stays near 9.8 at every inhibitor level while apparent Km rises roughly 1.3x, 2.1x and 5.2x at I = 0.5, 1 and 2 uM, which is competitive behaviour. That rise grows with I squared rather than linearly, so I fitted competitive inhibition with a Hill-type exponent (n ~ 2, Ki ~ 1 uM). These estimates are by hand inspection, not a formal regression, and the n = 2 form rests on only three inhibitor levels, so predictions at higher I are an extrapolation."}
